          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Reading the Bortle scale\n\nThe Bortle scale describes how a night sky looks to an observer. It runs from class 1, an exceptionally dark sky, to class 9, a bright inner-city sky. Lower numbers mean less artificial light. The scale is useful language for comparing places, but it is not a precise instrument reading and it cannot predict the weather.\n\nUnder a dark sky, faint stars become easier to see and the Milky Way may show structure instead of appearing as a barely visible band. Near a city, a bright background can wash out those details. You might still enjoy the Moon, planets and bright constellations. A high Bortle number does not mean there is nothing to see; it means the faintest parts of the sky have more competition.\n\nClasses 1 and 2 describe very dark skies. Class 3 is a rural sky where artificial light may be visible near the horizon. Class 4 is a transition toward suburban conditions. Classes 5 and 6 bring more skyglow, and classes 7 through 9 describe increasingly bright skies. These are broad descriptions, not a checklist that every observer will agree on perfectly.\n\nYour experience depends on more than artificial light. A full Moon can brighten an otherwise remote park. Haze and smoke scatter light. A cloud can reflect light from a town or hide the stars altogether. Trees, cliffs and mountains can block your view without changing the darkness of the open sky. Even your eyes need time to adjust after a bright screen or a car's headlights.\n\nNyx includes a conservative Bortle estimate for each park. It is a planning assumption, not a measurement made at the moment you arrive. A large park may contain very different viewing conditions. An estimate for the park cannot describe every campground, overlook or trail. This is why the app labels the class as an estimate and keeps weather, moonlight and the length of darkness separate in the score breakdown.\n\nWhen you record a journal entry, choose the class that seems closest to the sky you observed. There is no need to turn your evening into an examination. Note whether the Milky Way was visible, where a bright horizon appeared, and whether haze or the Moon changed the view. Those notes may be more useful on your next trip than the number alone.\n\nTry comparing the same place on different nights. A moonless evening after clear weather can feel very different from a humid night under a bright Moon. That difference is part of learning to read the sky. Use the scale to describe an experience, use the forecast to prepare, and give yourself time to look before deciding what you can see."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "essay.darkness": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A sky worth protecting\n\nA dark sky is more than a place to see stars. It is a habitat. Long before electric light, plants and animals lived with a steady pattern of bright days and dark nights. That rhythm still matters. A lamp beside a road may help a person see the ground, but its light can reach far beyond the road, into the places where other lives unfold.\n\nLight scattered in the atmosphere creates skyglow. From a city, it can hide the faint band of the Milky Way. The brightest stars remain, while the quieter parts of the sky disappear. A person who has never seen a truly dark night may not know what is missing. A national park can offer a first encounter with a sky that feels unexpectedly full.\n\nDarkness does not mean using no light. It means putting light where it helps, at the time it is needed, and at a level that suits the task. A shielded light aimed toward the ground serves a path better than one shining into the sky. A light switched off when nobody needs it gives darkness back to the surrounding landscape. Small decisions can matter when many people make them.\n\nPark staff work to protect night skies by improving outdoor lighting, studying sky brightness and helping visitors learn how to enjoy the night. International Dark Sky certification recognizes sustained work on lighting and education. It is not a promise that every night will be clear, or that every corner of a certified park will be equally dark. Clouds, the Moon, nearby towns and your own viewing location still shape the experience.\n\nNyx treats a park's Bortle class as an estimate because darkness is not a permanent property of a name on a map. A visitor center may have more light than a remote overlook. A bright horizon may affect one direction more than another. Smoke and haze can scatter light even on a night with little cloud. The score helps you choose a promising night; your eyes tell you what the night actually offers.\n\nYou can help protect that experience. Choose a modest red light for moving around. Turn off vehicle headlights when safely parked, and avoid shining a beam toward other observers. Ask a ranger where you may stay after dark. Leave paths, wildlife and cultural places as you found them.\n\nBack at home, look at your own outdoor lights. Consider shielding them, lowering their brightness or using a timer. You do not need a telescope to be a caretaker of the night. Sometimes the most useful thing you can do is let a light go out."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "essay.etiquette": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Sharing the night\n\nA quiet place under the stars is often a shared place. Some visitors have brought telescopes. Some are making photographs. Others are seeing the Milky Way for the first time. A little care helps everyone keep the darkness they came to find, and helps the park remain a home for wildlife after the daytime crowds leave.\n\nBegin by checking access. A park may be open at night while a particular road, overlook or visitor facility is closed. Seasonal conditions can change what is safe to reach. Read current alerts and ask a ranger before your trip. Nyx may have cached information, so a score never confirms that a road is open. If conditions are uncertain, choose a familiar, permitted viewing area rather than improvising a route in the dark.\n\nBring a small red light and use the lowest useful brightness. Even red light can be disruptive if it is aimed directly into someone's eyes. Point it at the ground when you need to move. Let your eyes adjust gradually. Avoid checking a bright phone screen; if you use Nyx's night-vision mode, also lower the device brightness to a comfortable level before you start observing.\n\nArrive before darkness when you can. It is easier to understand a parking area, find the path and notice changes in the ground while you can still see them clearly. Once safely parked, switch off lights you do not need. If you must leave during a star party, let people nearby know before turning on your headlights. Safety comes first, and a little notice protects everyone's patience.\n\nKeep voices low and give other observers room. Ask before touching a telescope or stepping into a photographer's setup. A tripod can be difficult to see at night. If someone offers a look through their eyepiece, follow their instructions and take your time. Many people who love the sky also enjoy helping someone else find their first planet or star cluster.\n\nStay on permitted surfaces. Desert soils, dune plants and cultural sites can be damaged by footsteps, even when the damage is difficult to see. Darkness is not permission to leave a trail. Do not approach wildlife or use a flashlight to hold an animal in view. Take your food, wrappers and other belongings with you.\n\nPrepare for the temperature to change, and carry what you need to be comfortable without relying on a phone connection. A plan shared with someone at home is useful when reception is poor. Let the night be unhurried. You do not need to see everything, and a cloudy evening can still offer quiet, cool air and the sound of the landscape. Leave the place ready for the next person to look up."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "essay.milkyway": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Finding the Milky Way\n\nThe Milky Way is our own galaxy seen from the inside: a band of light from countless distant stars, crossed by dark lanes of dust. Its brightest part, the galactic center, lies toward the constellations Sagittarius and Scorpius. Seeing it well depends on four things Nyx already weighs: a dark site, a dark Moon, clear air and enough true darkness.\n\nSeason matters most. From the United States the bright center rises before dawn in early spring, stands highest in the southern sky through June, July and August, and sinks into the southwest soon after dusk by October. From November through January it is close to the Sun and out of the night sky. In American Samoa, south of the equator, the center climbs nearly overhead in the same months. The fainter band still arches across the winter sky, through Cassiopeia and past Orion, and it rewards a dark night too.\n\nThe Moon decides which nights are worth the drive. A bright Moon washes out the faint glow, even at the darkest park. Look for nights near the new moon, or nights when the Moon sets early or rises late and leaves hours of true darkness. The calendar's ringed nights and the lighter, moonlit stretches of the sky arc show both.\n\nGive your eyes time. Full dark adaptation takes twenty to thirty minutes, and one glance at a bright screen undoes much of it. Turn on night-vision mode, lower your screen brightness, and use a dim red light on the trail.\n\nIn summer, look low and to the south, and choose a place with an open southern horizon: a desert flat, a ridgeline overlook, a lake shore. Cliffs, trees and canyon walls can hide the brightest part even under a perfect sky. Haze and wildfire smoke dim it as surely as clouds do, which is one reason no forecast can promise what you will see.\n\nPhotographs show more color and detail than the eye can. To the eye, the Milky Way is a soft, silvery river with darker gaps. That is not a disappointment. It is the real thing: light that has travelled for thousands of years to reach you."
          }
        }
      }
    },
